==Norwegian Bokmål==
{{wikipedia|lang=no|S}}

===Etymology===
The lowercase form of {{m|nb|S}}. Its modern rounded shape developed from cursive and minuscule forms of Latin {{m|la|S}}. In medieval and early modern writing, round ⟨s⟩ coexisted with long ⟨ſ⟩; long ⟨ſ⟩ was generally used initially and medially, while round ⟨s⟩ was normally used word-finally. The round form eventually became standard in all positions as long ⟨ſ⟩ fell out of general use around the beginning of the 19th century.

The Latin letter was adopted through Etruscan {{m|ett|𐌔|tr=s}} from a western form of {{der|nb|grc|Σ|tr=S|t=sigma}}, ultimately from {{der|nb|phn|𐤔|tr=š|t=šīn; tooth}}. The Norwegian letter name ''ess'' ultimately continues Latin {{m|la|es|t=the name of the letter S}}.

The unit symbol {{m|nb|s}} is taken from the initial letter of {{m|nb|sekund|t=second}}.

===Pronunciation===
